{"default":"The fruits (*dʒo*) are basically used as an edible item. Jams and sherbet made from the fruits (*dʒo*) are a delicacy in many regions.

**Medicinal values for:** *fever and sore throat.*

**Fruit:** The fruit (*dʒo*) juice (*ros* or *rasa*) is also taken as a traditional medicine in case of mild fever (*rua*) and sore throat."}

{"default":"**Medicinal values for:** *malaria*

Since the plant is notorious for the extreme itchiness it produces on contact, particularly with the young foliage and the seed (*dʒaŋ*) pods it is not primarily preferred for medicinal uses. However, it is known to the Birhor from their ancestors that the root (*rehet*) crushed (*kʰeʧaʔ*) with water (*daʔ*) and black pepper (*golki*) given once or twice in a day is a good medicine for curing malaria.

**Interesting fact:** To get rid of the itchiness caused by the young foliage and seed (*dʒaŋ*) pods of **babat buna**, the person needs to apply cow dung (*guriʧ*) all over the infected area."}

{"default":"**Sakarkanda** is basically used for edible purposes by the Birhor community, as well as a couple of medicinal uses. 

**Medicinal values for:** *digestive problems, constipation, inflammation*

**Tuber:** It is considered a good support for digestive health. It prevents constipation. It also helps reduce inflammation. They boil (*hõɽeʧ*) it and peel (*ʧʰolaʔ*) the skin and eat it."}
